{
  "dom_id": "question-182604",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{n}^{2}(n+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{n}^{2}(n+1)^{2}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
